 and criminal prosecutions.","evidence":"amend title 18, United States Code, to ensure that law enforcement has the legal tools necessary to combat organized retail crime","evidence_verified":true,"key":"magnitude","score":30},{"assessment":"Establishes a temporary coordination center within the Department of Homeland Security with multi-agency staff and reporting requirements, but states no specific funding amounts.","evidence":"establish the Organized Retail and Supply Chain Crime Coordination Center","evidence_verified":true,"key":"fiscal","score":25},{"assessment":"Amends federal criminal code regarding stolen property, money laundering, and cargo theft, and creates a new interagency coordination center within DHS.","evidence":"Part I of title 18, United States Code, is amended","evidence_verified":true,"key":"scope","score":35},{"assessment":"Most criminal code amendments are permanent, while the newly established coordination center has a statutory sunset of seven years.","evidence":"The authority of the Center shall terminate on the date that is 7 years after the date on which the Center is established","evidence_verified":true,"key":"duration","score":30}],"missing_information":["Specific funding authorization or budget amounts for the new coordination center."],"rationale":"The bill expands federal criminal enforcement capabilities for organized retail crime, establishes a temporary coordination center within the Department of Homeland Security, and increases investigative tools such as forfeiture and money laundering provisions, resulting in limited to moderate legal and administrative impacts.","score":35},"importance_meta":{"generated_at":"2026-10-26T22:24:10+00:00","model":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite","source_basis":"text+summary","source_urls":["https://www.congress.gov/119/bills/hr2853/BILLS-119hr2853rfs.htm"]},"introduced_date":"2025-04-10","is_demo":false,"label":"H.R. 2853","latest_action":{"date":"2026-05-13","text":"Received in the Senate and Read twi